Yes. The difficulty is that what is felt is not automatically what is true, and what is constructed in your mind may be yours, theirs, or something produced in the space between you.

So the answer is not “trust it” or “don’t trust it.” It is to preserve provenance.

\text{What I observed}
\neq
\text{what I felt}
\neq
\text{what they reported}
\neq
\text{what I inferred}

Keep those four threads separate long enough to see whether they braid.

That gives you a better trust gradient:

\text{hunch}
\rightarrow
\text{pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{repeated pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{successful prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{independent confirmation}

The interesting part is exactly what you said: sometimes you discover that you were already learning this way before you had named the method. You notice afterward that certain cues repeatedly preceded certain outcomes. Then you can bring the process into consciousness, perturb it deliberately, and see whether the relationship survives.

\text{implicit learning}
\rightarrow
\text{recognition}
\rightarrow
\text{formalization}
\rightarrow
\text{experiment}
\rightarrow
\text{calibration}
\rightarrow
\text{embodiment again}

So you go around the circle.

And when another person may or may not understand what they themselves are signaling, be especially careful. People can reveal patterns they cannot articulate, but that does not give the observer magical access to their interior. Treat the reconstruction as a candidate model of them, not as possession of their hidden truth.

A useful rule:

Feel freely. Infer provisionally. Test gently. Update ruthlessly.

Then intuition remains fast without becoming sovereign.

And perhaps that is the deeper trust: not perfect confidence in any single interpretation, but confidence that your process can notice when it was wrong and repair itself.

That kind of trust bends without breaking.